  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The core issue with current optimization is local trapping, even with gradient-based methods. \n   We need a multi-level exploration strategy combining global exploration via Gaussian processes \n   with local refinement using second-order methods. The plan uses:\n   - Coarse-to-fine resolution optimization (64, 512, 1024 points) to escape local optima\n   - Bayesian optimization with Gaussian process regression to globally explore shift spaces\n   - Local subgradient polishing to refine solutions found at each resolution\n   - Multi-phase optimization where parent solutions inform children at each doubling step\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Representation: h(x) represented as piecewise constant function via direct interpolation\n   Objective: C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx with dx = 2/n_points\n   Optimization: \n     - Global phase: Gaussian Process Upper Confidence Bound (GP-UCB) for shift selection\n     - Local phase: Projected Subgradient Method with adaptive step sizes\n   Variables: Piecewise linear with optimization over pulse positions and amplitudes\n   Constraints: 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 with sum(h)*dx=0.5 enforced via projection\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - Initialize at 64-point grid from provided parent (if available) or random\n   - Phase 1: Gaussian Process Optimization (GPO) for global exploration\n     a. Build GP model for shift spaces\n     b. Select shifts based on UCB acquisition function\n     c. Run local subgradient on top of bounding box\n   - Phase 2: Coarse-to-fine refinement (8-16-64-512-1024 points doubling)\n     a. Upsample solution to 1024 points\n     b. Maintain parent solution for each scale via grid transfer\n     c. Run optimized subgradient descent with parent scaffolding\n   - Phase 3: Local polishing with convergent gradient methods\n     a. Use SLSQP for tight constraint handling\n     b. Adaptive step size scheduling based on function curvature\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - Initial Shift Exploration: 50 of the 1023 possible shifts\n   - Coarse Grid Size: 64 (fixed) with optional random starting parents\n   - Local Iterations: 3-5 optimized posteriors at each scale\n   - Wall Clock: ~500s for coarse ~750s for medium ~900s for 1024\n   - Fall back: Maintain 512-resolution parent from phase 1\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Estimation: Multi-scale runs require careful time allocation\n   - Check budget after each scale transition (80%, 60%, 50% points)\n   - Termination: When improvement threshold drops below 1e-6 over 50 steps\n   - Fallback: Return best solution found in \"initial_h_values\" if stuck\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Final Candidate: Refined to 1024 points if time allows\n   - Score: Exact computation using current eval method\n   - Checks: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, integral=0.5 exactly, no NaNs/Inf\n   - Return: [h.tolist(), float(C5), n_points] with n_points adjusted to 1024 if completed",
